    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"goblin's gold\" effect, my friend, is a metaphorical expression I use to describe how goblins, when they're not busy causing mischief, are often drawn to shiny, valuable treasures. In this case, the yellowing leaves on your tomato plants are like the discarded, wilted leaves that goblins leave behind, while the healthy, green leaves are like the treasure they covet.\n\nNow, back to fixing the soil. If you suspect a nitrogen deficiency, you can try adding a nitrogen-rich fertilizer, such as ammonium nitrate or urea. However, it's essential to note that overfertilizing can be just as detrimental as underfertilizing. You want to strike a balance, like a goblin carefully selecting the perfect gemstone from a pile of rough, unpolished rocks.\n\nTo adjust the pH levels, you can use a pH test kit to determine the current pH level of your soil. Most tomato plants prefer a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, ranging from 6.0 to 7.0. If your soil is too acidic, you can add lime to raise the pH. Conversely, if it's too alkaline, you can add elemental sulfur to lower the pH.\n\nIt",
